<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Formulating inequalities for a dietary supplement program</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A dairy farmer in Nyandarua mixes \( x \) kg of maize germ with \( y \) kg of sunflower cake to feed the herd each day.</p>
<ul>
<li>The cows can eat at most <b>{@W@} kg</b> of the mixture in a day.</li>
<li>Each kg of maize germ provides <b>{@A@} units</b> of protein, and each kg of sunflower cake provides <b>{@B@} units</b>.</li>
<li>The herd needs <b>at least {@N@} units</b> of protein each day.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>a) </b> Write the two linear inequalities that model these conditions. <i>(Use \( \le \) or \( \ge \) as appropriate. Do not include \(x \ge 0\) and \(y \ge 0\).)</i></p>

<p>Amount the cows can eat: [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>Protein needed: [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p><b>b) </b> One week the farmer cannot buy any maize germ, so \(x = 0\).</p>
<p>Using your protein inequality, what is the smallest amount of sunflower cake (\(y\)) that meets the herd's protein need?</p>
<p>Smallest amount of sunflower cake: [[input:ans3]] kg [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p><b>Part a: Forming the constraints</b></p>
<p>1. <b>Amount the cows can eat:</b> the total mass of the mixture is \(x + y\) kg, and it can be at most \({@W@}\) kg. "At most" means less than or equal to (\( \le \)):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{{@ta1@}} \)</p>
<p>2. <b>Protein needed:</b> the protein from the maize germ is \({@A*x@}\) units and from the sunflower cake \({@B*y@}\) units. Together they must be at least \({@N@}\). "At least" means greater than or equal to (\( \ge \)):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{{@ta2@}} \)</p>
<p><b>Part b: When there is no maize germ</b></p>
<p>Substitute \( x = 0 \) into the protein inequality and solve for \(y\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">
\( \displaystyle \begin{aligned}
{@A*x+B*y@} &amp;\ge {@N@} \\
{@B*y@} &amp;\ge {@N@} \\
y &amp;\ge {@ta3@}
\end{aligned} \)
</p>
<p>So the farmer needs at least <b>{@ta3@} kg</b> of sunflower cake. This is within the limit of \({@W@}\) kg the cows can eat, so it is possible.</p>
<p>The graph shows both conditions; every point in the shaded region is a mixture that works.</p>
<div style="text-align:center;" role="img" aria-label="Graph of x + y at most {#W#} and the protein condition, with the region that satisfies both shaded.">
[[jsxgraph width="380px" height="340px"]]
(function() {
  var X = {#W*1.05#}, Y = {#W*1.05#}, C = [[1,1,{#W#},true,false],[{#A#},{#B#},{#N#},false,false]];
  var b = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {boundingbox:[-X*0.12, Y*1.1, X*1.1, -Y*0.2], axis:true, grid:false,
      showNavigation:false, showCopyright:false, pan:{enabled:false}, zoom:{enabled:false}});
  // clip the first-quadrant box by each half-plane (Sutherland-Hodgman)
  var poly = [[0,0],[X,0],[X,Y],[0,Y]];
  function inside(p, c) { var v = c[0]*p[0] + c[1]*p[1] - c[2]; return c[3] ? v <= 1e-9 : v >= -1e-9; }
  function cut(p, q, c) { var fp = c[0]*p[0] + c[1]*p[1] - c[2], fq = c[0]*q[0] + c[1]*q[1] - c[2], t = fp/(fp - fq);
    return [p[0] + t*(q[0]-p[0]), p[1] + t*(q[1]-p[1])]; }
  for (var k = 0; k < C.length; k++) {
    var out = [];
    for (var i = 0; i < poly.length; i++) {
      var p = poly[i], q = poly[(i+1) % poly.length], ip = inside(p, C[k]), iq = inside(q, C[k]);
      if (ip) out.push(p);
      if (ip !== iq) out.push(cut(p, q, C[k]));
    }
    poly = out;
  }
  if (poly.length > 2) b.create('polygon', poly, {fillColor:'#a5d6a7', fillOpacity:0.55, vertices:{visible:false},
      borders:{visible:false}, fixed:true});
  var cols = ['#1565c0', '#c62828', '#6a1b9a'];
  for (var k = 0; k < C.length; k++) {
    var c = C[k];
    b.create('line', [[c[1] === 0 ? c[2]/c[0] : 0, c[1] === 0 ? 0 : c[2]/c[1]], [c[1] === 0 ? c[2]/c[0] : 1, c[1] === 0 ? 1 : (c[2]-c[0])/c[1]]],
      {strokeColor:cols[k % 3], strokeWidth:2, dash: c[4] ? 2 : 0, fixed:true});
  }
  b.create('text', [X*1.08, -Y*0.15, 'maize germ x'], {fontSize:13, fixed:true, anchorX:'right'});
  b.create('text', [X*0.02, Y*1.06, 'sunflower cake y'], {fontSize:13, fixed:true});
})();
[[/jsxgraph]]
</div>]]></text>
